<h2>Tropical storms and hurricanes</h2>
<p>Kingsville is inland, but tropical systems still reach it. On September 7, 2010, Tropical Storm Hermine knocked down tree limbs and power lines across Kleberg County and <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=260836" target="_blank" rel="noopener">blew the roof off a local business in Kingsville</a>, with a 63 mph gust at NAS Kingsville. When Hurricane Hanna came ashore on Padre Island in July 2020, the Baffin Bay monitoring site and radar showed <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=912707" target="_blank" rel="noopener">hurricane force gusts in coastal Kleberg County</a>, and surge damaged many piers at Loyola Beach and Riviera Beach. During Tropical Storm Harold on August 22, 2023, <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1133834" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trees were blown down on the Texas A&amp;M University-Kingsville campus</a>, and Loyola Beach recorded a 67 mph gust.</p>
<p>Wind-driven rain from these storms often shows up later as ceiling stains, wet insulation, or mold. Our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/mold-damage-claims/">mold damage claims</a> pages explain how those losses are typically handled.</p>

<h2>TWIA, flood, and local rules</h2>
<p>Kleberg County is in the TWIA designated catastrophe area. TWIA covers wind and hail only, TWIA claims generally must be filed within one year of the date of loss, and properties built, altered, or repaired on or after January 1, 1988 need a windstorm certificate of compliance for TWIA eligibility. Re-roofs and roof repairs fall under the TDI Windstorm Inspection Program. Many inland Kingsville homeowners instead have wind and hail on a standard homeowners policy, often with a separate wind/hail deductible; our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">deductible calculator</a> can help you estimate yours.</p>
<p>Flood coverage comes from the NFIP or a private flood policy, generally after a 30-day waiting period. FEMA data (April 2025) lists Kleberg County as an NFIP participant without CRS status. City of Kingsville permit rules are set locally; check with the city before work begins.</p>

<h2>Claim issues we often see in Kingsville</h2>
<p>Kleberg County's NCEI history includes 64 thunderstorm wind reports (29 of them in May), 30 tornado reports, and 61 flash flood reports, so Kingsville claims come from many directions. Older pier-and-beam homes can take wind-driven rain through siding and windows as well as the roof. Large trees bring down limbs on roofs, fences, and vehicles. Manufactured homes are especially vulnerable to large hail and downbursts. And student rental properties raise questions about who insures what: the landlord's policy usually covers the building, while tenants need renters insurance for their own belongings.</p>
<p>If an insurer's inspection seems rushed or leaves out damaged elevations, our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> and <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claims</a> pages explain the next steps a policy may allow.</p>

<h2>How we help</h2>
